Date: Fri, 29 Oct 1993 10:06:05 +0000 (GMT)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: EXTROPIAN CORRECTNESS

pgf@srl02.cacs.usl.edu (Phil G. Fraering) writes:

>>Some forms of technology are inetivable, like computer networks,
>>Cryptography, etc. But others, like for example pesticides, need
>>a lot more thinking through before we apply them willy-nilly as 
>>we have in the past.

>If you think that computer networks and cryptography are "inevitable"
>but that pesticides need "consideration" before allowing legal
>use, you must be having severe problems with your internal logic
>circuitry. Run a diagnostic at once.

With all due respect Phil, don't be silly.  The original point, though
it could have been phrased more acutely, is a valid one.  There's a
set of good applications, and a set of bad applications, for any
technology; likewise, there are good side-effects and bad
side-effects.  For example, cheap crypto promises to give us personal
security, freedom from interference, and autonomy; but among its bad
applications I'd include items like assassination markets, and among
its bad side-effects I'd list things like provoking a government
clamp-down on free speech in the new media.

By thinking through the possible side-effect carefully before making
use of a new tool, we minimize the risk of landing ourselves with the
shit.  In the past there has often been a tendency to see only the
good, desired applications of a new technology (like pesticides)
without thinking through things like, "but how do we get rid of it
after its done its job?"  or, "can this substance also be used as a
nerve gas in warfare?"

Foresight, Phil. Foresight. 

And to drag things back a bit further, to the origin of the debate:
yeah, IMHO Sagan was _completely_ right to stand up and start shouting
when he realized there was a risk of a "nuclear winter".  If it was a
real risk it damn well ought to have been made public.  Whether he
should have kept quiet and re-checked his sums is a different question;
he's probably guilty of sloppy science (if, that is, all the facts now
known were available to him.) Rifkind is a different fish entirely,
who -- rather than evaluating possible threats and then publicizing
them -- campaigns for blanket bans on anything that his ignorance
makes him afraid of.




-- Charlie
