From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: QUANTUM: Many Minds Interpretation

Paul Cisek writes in X-Message-Number: #93-10-885:
>
>>Take a pair of spin one-half particles which have been created together
>>such that conservation of angular momentum requires their 'spins' to be
>>opposite.  [...]  Since the first three combinations all have
>>correlations of under 15% it follows that the maximum correlation
>>in the final L=0, R=135 combination of settings must still be under
>>3 * 15%, or 45%, provided the choice of measurement on one cannot
>>influence the results obtained from the other.
>
>Perhaps I'm incredibly dense, but this still does not make sense to me.  Why
>should three out of four cases with two 1/2-spin particles expect to correlate
>at ~15% and the fourth to correlate at 85% ?  What is meant by "opposite" spin?
>(Your discussion suggests that 0 and 45 degrees are opposite - I'd guess it
>should be 0 and 90...)  Perhaps my curiosity would best be satisfied with some
>references...

It seemed a bit of a cop-out to just say "read these books:" without
attempting any explanation.  I've listed a few references below, anyway.

If you've got a 1/2 spin particle such as a proton, let's say you test
it for vertically up spin (call it 0 degrees).  It tests positive.  But
this has only given us one bit of information; it still exists in a state
which is a superposition of possible spins ranging between sideways left
and right (270 thru 0 thru 90 degrees).  To properly describe the
superposition you need tools like Argand planes, Hilbert space and
Riemann spheres.  But I'm pretty sure that at the level of a single
universe common sense and underlying objective reality reappear, and
each particle does have a precise spin axis, position and momentum.  

So testing the "up" particle a second time for vertically up, the chance
of getting positive is 100%.  Opposite spin is 180 degrees (90 for photons)
-- the 45 degrees is just how the measurements are set in the thought
experiment.  If we test for vertically down (180 degrees), as this is
opposite it has 0% chance of testing positive.  A test for 5 degrees off
from original vertically up gives a probability of 0.5(1 + cos(5 degrees))
= 0.9981 of getting a positive, decreasing as we extend the angle.  And
the quantum probabilities, the results you actually get, are as according
to this formula.

But in the thought experiment we're measuring two particles of opposite 
spin, so we have to remember to subtract the angle from 180 degrees before
applying the formula.  Test one for a straight 'up' and one for a straight
'down' and it's certain that you get 2 yeses or 2 noes.

We had four possible ways of setting the measurement -- in three of them
the measurements for left and right travelling particle were set 45
degrees apart, in the other it was 135 degrees which explains the
difference.  As for the bit about correlations not being able to add up to
more than 45% without superluminal influences here's an example I used to
prove it to myself:-

135 deg.  R'   N Y Y N Y N N N N Y Y Y Y N Y Y Y Y N N Y
90  deg.  L    Y N N Y N Y Y Y N Y Y N N Y N N N N Y Y N
45  deg.  R    N Y Y N N N Y N Y N N Y Y N Y Y N Y N N Y
0   deg.  L'   N Y Y Y Y Y N Y N Y Y N N Y N N Y N Y Y N

               C C C   C   C   C C C           C         9 / 21 < 45% 
 
We started with the settings at L=90 and R=45 which are the middle two
lines above.  The correlations are a little under 15%, so we'll say 3
in a run of 21, as in the example.  Now change the L setting to 0, so
we've got the bottom two lines R and L' (notice R is not allowed to
change as this would require superluminal influences and violation of
Special Relativity).  The correlation is still about 3 in every 21.
If the R setting had been changed to 135 and L kept at 90 this gives
us results like the top two lines, again with 3 correlations.  But the
final combination, lines one and four, are 135 degrees apart, so would
actually give >85% correlation according to the quantum probabilities.
If the state of one isn't allowed to jump depending on the measurement
settings of the other, we can only get < 45% correlation as in the
example, which is contradicted by the actual results of experiments.
So there are superluminal influences.   QED.
  
At least, that's what those who deny the existence of Many Worlds have
to conclude -- they think that minds don't "collapse" themselves, but can
"collapse" wavefunctions of inanimate matter.  In other words, minds are 
able to "drive a coach and six" through the laws of inanimate matter.  

I've mostly referred to Penrose's *The Emperor's New Mind* (not 
Extropianly Correct for his views on consciousness, but I think rather
useful for the physics and maths content.  Lots of interesting stuff.)
ISBN 0-09-977170-5 

Another recommended book is John Gribbin's *In Search of Schrodinger's
Cat*  ISBN 0-552-12555-5  All devoted to "Quantum Physics and Reality",
and Gribbin is a Many Worldist.  Not as much detail as ENM above.

Then we have Paul Davies' *Superforce*  ISBN 0-04-539006-1 
and *The Arrow of Time* (Peter Coveney & Roger Highfield)
ISBN 0-00-654462-2 (this last one only has about 40 pages on QM).
* The Quantum Universe* (Tony Hey & Patrick Walters) ISBN 0-521-31845-9
runs to 160 pages but doesn't mention Aspect's work.

>What does Many Worlds suggest?  Again, I don't understand why my multiversal
>mind should choose realities where Alain Aspect happened to get the results he
>did.  What has happened to my diverging twins?

Immediately the particles are generated the experimenter's mind is in a
superposition, a multiverse of universes which yield a superposition of
all possible spin states for the particle pair.  Every time the spin of
a pair of particles is measured, the experimenter's mind diverges and
jumps into a particular state.  It could have been any of the possible
states (and it was, somewhere in reality space).  But the result, either
way, is that the experimenter finds that the results statistically agree
with the predictions made by quantum theory.  The experimenter's mind
might have ended up in another state, with a different sequence of yeses
and noes, but statistically the results would be similar.  What can't
possibly happen is that the experimenter's mind stays in an "uncollapsed
state" of significantly distinctive superpositions after the observation
has been made, as this would require telepathy.  We never hear of someone
who sees a superposition of dead and live cats (although you could have
someone existing in a multiverse where a cat was both dead and alive,
provided the observer's brain had been wired up in one of the states to
falsely think that the converse case was true). 

Then a secondary observer (you for instance) comes onto the scene, and
observes the experimenter or hears of his results.  And the same
divergence occurs.  You don't have to end up in a reality where the
sequence of yeses and noes in Aspect's results were exactly as they
occured in our reality.  Although statistically things would be the same
over each large sequence of measurements, and your diverging twins are
bound to end up in a reality where he got some particular set of results.
They can't end up thinking that he didn't get the results he got!  :-)

